course: dictionary
lesson: 4935
title: Words that beg
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words say more than their simple meaning. «Implore» means to beg someone very strongly and with feeling. «I implore you, please don't go out on the ice.» The speaker is not just asking. The words «please» and «I implore» show that they are worried and really want the listener to listen.
explain: Check the clues around the word. «Toad's friends implore him to give up motor-cars.» The friends want Toad to stop something that is causing trouble, and they feel strongly about it. So implore must mean beg, not just ask. Try the swap: «Toad's friends beg him to give up motor-cars» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Notice how strong the word is. «Ask» is a plain, everyday word. «Implore» is a stronger, more formal word, and you will often find it in stories when a character is desperate. If you read «implored him to stop», you know the character is not calm about it.
fact: Implore means to beg someone very strongly and with feeling.
fact: «Implored» is the past form, as in «She implored him to stop».
fact: Implore is stronger than ask, and it is a more formal word.
fact: Words that are close in meaning to implore include beg, plead and entreat.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad's friends implore him to give up motor-cars.
exercise: «I implore you, please don't go out on the ice.» What does implore mean here? | *To beg very strongly | To laugh loudly at a joke | To walk away quietly | To count the steps slowly
exercise: «Toad's friends implore him to give up motor-cars.» What does this tell you about how the friends feel? | *They feel strongly and want Toad to stop | They are happy and want to sing | They are bored and want to go home | They are tired and want to sleep
exercise: Which word from the story means the same as begged? | heartiness | *implored | ancient | castle
test: «The Elephant's Child implored his relatives to tell him what the Crocodile eats.» Why would the Elephant's Child implore them rather than just ask? | He is singing a song to his relatives | He is bored and does not really care about the answer | He is asleep and cannot speak | *He is eager and desperate to know, so he begs with feeling
test: Which word could replace implore in «She implored him to stop» and keep the meaning? | *begged | counted | slept | laughed
exam: Jayden asks, «Is implore just another word for ask?» What is the best answer? | No, implore means to sing loudly | *No, implore is stronger, showing someone begs with strong feeling | Yes, but only when someone is sleepy | Yes, they mean exactly the same thing with no difference
